@lang en
IMPORT prelude

-- ca-interpretation-resolutions.l4
--
-- The Interpretation Act (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. I-21: section 39(1), the four expressions "subject to affirmative resolution of
-- Parliament", "subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons", "subject to negative resolution of Parliament" and "subject to
-- negative resolution of the House of Commons".  Pass 3 (2026-10-29), written after an independent test pass asked questions the time module
-- could not answer (which House, annulment, the negative expressions).
-- Imports the standard library only.  The time module keeps the rule for laying a regulation within fifteen days (it is the same for all
-- four expressions: `the regulation made on` d `may be laid on` e `given the days the body sits` s), and the repeal module keeps section 39(2)
-- (what an annulment does).  This module answers the three things section 39(1) says that differ from one expression to the next.
--
-- WHAT EACH EXPRESSION "MEANS", FROM THE TEXT (ss 39(1)(a) to (d), lines 376 to 379).  Each paragraph says that the regulation
--   * "shall be laid before" a body (Parliament for (a) and (c), the House of Commons for (b) and (d)), within fifteen days;
--   * and then, for (a) and (b): "shall not come into force unless and until it is affirmed by a resolution of both Houses of Parliament"
--     (a) or "of the House of Commons" (b);
--   * or, for (c) and (d): "may be annulled by a resolution of both Houses of Parliament" (c) or "of the House of Commons" (d).
-- "Means" gives the whole meaning, so what a paragraph does not say is not part of the expression: (c) and (d) put no condition on coming
-- into force, and a resolution of one House does not do what (a) or (c) requires of both.
-- A "resolution of both Houses" is read as a resolution passed by each House (the Senate and the House of Commons).  Both are named in the text
-- (line 38, line 144); the booleans below are "the Senate has passed one" and "the House of Commons has passed one".
--
-- THE HOUSE RULE FOR A RULE THAT HAS A STATED CONDITION.  TRUE where the text states the condition and it is met; FALSE where the text
-- says "means" (so what is not in the meaning is out) or says "shall not ... unless"; a refusal where the case is not one the text addresses.

§ `Section 39(1): the four expressions and the body each names`

DECLARE `An expression of section 39(1)` IS ONE OF
    `subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament`
    `subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons`
    `subject to negative resolution of Parliament`
    `subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons`

DECLARE `A body that a regulation is laid before` IS ONE OF
    `Parliament`
    `the House of Commons`

-- Paragraphs (a) and (c) lay the regulation "before Parliament"; paragraphs (b) and (d) "before the House of Commons".
-- src:376 | (a) the expression subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before Parliament within fifteen days after it is made or, if Parliament is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that Parliament is sitting and shall not come into force unless and until it is affirmed by a resolution of both Houses of Parliament introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of those Houses;
-- src:377 | (b) the expression subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before the House of Commons within fifteen days after it is made or, if the House is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that the House is sitting and shall not come into force unless and until it is affirmed by a resolution of the House of Commons introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of that House;
-- src:378 | (c) the expression subject to negative resolution of Parliament, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before Parliament within fifteen days after it is made or, if Parliament is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that Parliament is sitting and may be annulled by a resolution of both Houses of Parliament introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of those Houses; and
-- src:379 | (d) the expression subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before the House of Commons within fifteen days after it is made or, if the House is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that the House is sitting and may be annulled by a resolution of the House of Commons introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of that House.
@ref Interpretation Act s 39(1)(a) (src:376), s 39(1)(b) (src:377), s 39(1)(c) (src:378) and s 39(1)(d) (src:379)
GIVEN e IS AN `An expression of section 39(1)`
GIVETH A `A body that a regulation is laid before`
`the body before which a regulation must be laid under` e MEANS
    CONSIDER e
    WHEN `subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament`                   THEN `Parliament`
    WHEN `subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons`         THEN `the House of Commons`
    WHEN `subject to negative resolution of Parliament`                      THEN `Parliament`
    WHEN `subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons`            THEN `the House of Commons`

§ `Section 39(1): which resolutions an expression requires`

-- Whether the resolution(s) the expression names have been passed.  For "Parliament" that is "both Houses": the Senate and the House of Commons
-- each pass one.  For "the House of Commons" it is the House of Commons alone; whether the Senate has also passed one does not matter, because
-- the paragraph does not mention the Senate.
-- src:376 | (a) the expression subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before Parliament within fifteen days after it is made or, if Parliament is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that Parliament is sitting and shall not come into force unless and until it is affirmed by a resolution of both Houses of Parliament introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of those Houses;
-- src:377 | (b) the expression subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before the House of Commons within fifteen days after it is made or, if the House is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that the House is sitting and shall not come into force unless and until it is affirmed by a resolution of the House of Commons introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of that House;
-- src:378 | (c) the expression subject to negative resolution of Parliament, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before Parliament within fifteen days after it is made or, if Parliament is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that Parliament is sitting and may be annulled by a resolution of both Houses of Parliament introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of those Houses; and
-- src:379 | (d) the expression subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before the House of Commons within fifteen days after it is made or, if the House is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that the House is sitting and may be annulled by a resolution of the House of Commons introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of that House.
@ref Interpretation Act s 39(1)(a) (src:376), s 39(1)(b) (src:377), s 39(1)(c) (src:378) and s 39(1)(d) (src:379)
GIVEN e IS AN `An expression of section 39(1)`
      `the Senate has passed the resolution` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the House of Commons has passed the resolution` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the resolutions that the expression` e `names have been passed, given that the Senate has passed one is` `the Senate has passed the resolution` `and the House of Commons has passed one is` `the House of Commons has passed the resolution` MEANS
    CONSIDER e
    WHEN `subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament`                   THEN `the Senate has passed the resolution` AND `the House of Commons has passed the resolution`
    WHEN `subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons`         THEN `the House of Commons has passed the resolution`
    WHEN `subject to negative resolution of Parliament`                      THEN `the Senate has passed the resolution` AND `the House of Commons has passed the resolution`
    WHEN `subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons`            THEN `the House of Commons has passed the resolution`

§ `Section 39(1)(a) and (b): coming into force depends on affirmation`

-- (a) and (b) say that the regulation "shall not come into force unless and until it is affirmed"; (c) and (d) say nothing of the kind.
-- src:376 | (a) the expression subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before Parliament within fifteen days after it is made or, if Parliament is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that Parliament is sitting and shall not come into force unless and until it is affirmed by a resolution of both Houses of Parliament introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of those Houses;
-- src:377 | (b) the expression subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before the House of Commons within fifteen days after it is made or, if the House is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that the House is sitting and shall not come into force unless and until it is affirmed by a resolution of the House of Commons introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of that House;
-- src:378 | (c) the expression subject to negative resolution of Parliament, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before Parliament within fifteen days after it is made or, if Parliament is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that Parliament is sitting and may be annulled by a resolution of both Houses of Parliament introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of those Houses; and
-- src:379 | (d) the expression subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before the House of Commons within fifteen days after it is made or, if the House is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that the House is sitting and may be annulled by a resolution of the House of Commons introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of that House.
@ref Interpretation Act s 39(1)(a) (src:376), s 39(1)(b) (src:377), s 39(1)(c) (src:378) and s 39(1)(d) (src:379)
GIVEN e IS AN `An expression of section 39(1)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the coming into force of a regulation depends on a resolution if it is` e MEANS
    CONSIDER e
    WHEN `subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament`                   THEN TRUE
    WHEN `subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons`         THEN TRUE
    WHEN `subject to negative resolution of Parliament`                      THEN FALSE
    WHEN `subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons`            THEN FALSE

-- Whether section 39(1) leaves the way open for the regulation to have come into force.  For (a) and (b): only once the resolution(s) the
-- expression names have been passed (and, as the time module records, the day on which it then comes into force is not stated: fork F-39-AFFIRM).
-- A resolution of the House of Commons alone does not meet (a), which requires both Houses (#569).  For (c) and (d): there is no condition,
-- so section 39(1) leaves the way open whatever resolutions have been passed.
-- src:376 | (a) the expression subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before Parliament within fifteen days after it is made or, if Parliament is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that Parliament is sitting and shall not come into force unless and until it is affirmed by a resolution of both Houses of Parliament introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of those Houses;
-- src:377 | (b) the expression subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before the House of Commons within fifteen days after it is made or, if the House is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that the House is sitting and shall not come into force unless and until it is affirmed by a resolution of the House of Commons introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of that House;
@ref Interpretation Act s 39(1)(a) (src:376) and s 39(1)(b) (src:377)
GIVEN e IS AN `An expression of section 39(1)`
      `the Senate has passed the resolution` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the House of Commons has passed the resolution` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a regulation can have come into force as far as section 39(1) is concerned, being subject to` e `where the Senate has passed an affirming resolution is` `the Senate has passed the resolution` `and the House of Commons has passed one is` `the House of Commons has passed the resolution` MEANS
    IF   `the coming into force of a regulation depends on a resolution if it is` e
    THEN `the resolutions that the expression` e `names have been passed, given that the Senate has passed one is` `the Senate has passed the resolution` `and the House of Commons has passed one is` `the House of Commons has passed the resolution`
    ELSE TRUE

§ `Section 39(1)(c) and (d): annulment`

-- (c) and (d) say that the regulation "may be annulled by a resolution of both Houses of Parliament" (c) or "of the House of Commons" (d).
-- Annulled when the resolution(s) the expression names have been passed; not annulled otherwise, because the paragraph "means" that and
-- no more (so a resolution of the House of Commons alone does not annul a regulation under (c), #573, and one of the House of Commons
-- does annul it under (d), #574).
-- Paragraphs (a) and (b) say nothing of annulment, so for an expression of affirmative resolution the Act does not answer.
-- What an annulment does (the regulation is deemed revoked on the day the resolution is passed) is section 39(2), in the repeal module.
-- src:378 | (c) the expression subject to negative resolution of Parliament, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before Parliament within fifteen days after it is made or, if Parliament is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that Parliament is sitting and may be annulled by a resolution of both Houses of Parliament introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of those Houses; and
-- src:379 | (d) the expression subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before the House of Commons within fifteen days after it is made or, if the House is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that the House is sitting and may be annulled by a resolution of the House of Commons introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of that House.
@ref Interpretation Act s 39(1)(c) (src:378) and s 39(1)(d) (src:379)
GIVEN e IS AN `An expression of section 39(1)`
      `the Senate has passed the resolution` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the House of Commons has passed the resolution` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a regulation is annulled, being subject to` e `where the Senate has passed an annulling resolution is` `the Senate has passed the resolution` `and the House of Commons has passed one is` `the House of Commons has passed the resolution` MEANS
    CONSIDER e
    WHEN `subject to negative resolution of Parliament` THEN
        `the resolutions that the expression` e `names have been passed, given that the Senate has passed one is` `the Senate has passed the resolution` `and the House of Commons has passed one is` `the House of Commons has passed the resolution`
    WHEN `subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons` THEN
        `the resolutions that the expression` e `names have been passed, given that the Senate has passed one is` `the Senate has passed the resolution` `and the House of Commons has passed one is` `the House of Commons has passed the resolution`
    WHEN `subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament` THEN
        `section 39(1) says that a regulation subject to negative resolution may be annulled by the resolution it names and says nothing of annulling one subject to affirmative resolution`
    WHEN `subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons` THEN
        `section 39(1) says that a regulation subject to negative resolution may be annulled by the resolution it names and says nothing of annulling one subject to affirmative resolution`

-- src:378 | (c) the expression subject to negative resolution of Parliament, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before Parliament within fifteen days after it is made or, if Parliament is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that Parliament is sitting and may be annulled by a resolution of both Houses of Parliament introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of those Houses; and
-- src:379 | (d) the expression subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons, when used in relation to any regulation, means that the regulation shall be laid before the House of Commons within fifteen days after it is made or, if the House is not then sitting, on any of the first fifteen days next thereafter that the House is sitting and may be annulled by a resolution of the House of Commons introduced and passed in accordance with the rules of that House.
@ref Interpretation Act s 39(1)(c) (src:378) and s 39(1)(d) (src:379): annulment of a regulation subject to affirmative resolution is not addressed
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 39(1) says that a regulation subject to negative resolution may be annulled by the resolution it names and says nothing of annulling one subject to affirmative resolution` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 39(1) says that a regulation subject to negative resolution may be annulled by the resolution it names and says nothing of annulling one subject to affirmative resolution"
